        "This website was developed to solve a real operational problem I observed while working part-time as a camera operator in a broadcast studio, alongside my master's studies. The studio runs a daily morning program that is streamed and distributed across multiple platforms, including a mobile app, website, and digital radio.\n\nWhile working in the control room, I closely observed how live and recorded programs were managed. Every day, the program starts at a fixed time. Before the broadcast, the producer sends all required video materials. During the program, the host speaks, videos are played in between, and everything must stay perfectly aligned with airtime so the show feels smooth and professional.\n\nIn the existing workflow, the host manually calculated timings. When videos were played, the remaining duration was visible in the live streaming software, but calculating the total duration of multiple videos and determining when the host should return on air was done mentally or using a calculator. Airtime calculations, countdowns, and timing coordination were handled manually in real time, which increased cognitive load and the risk of mistakes.\n\nFor recorded programs that needed to appear live, the workflow was even more complex. The host maintained a program sheet manually, writing down the order of segments, video durations, speaking time, and airtime calculations. Multiple tools were used at once, such as a notepad for the program order, a stopwatch to measure speaking duration, and separate timers to track total program time. This fragmented process made operations slower and harder to manage.\n\nAs a computing student with a background in observing real systems, I identified this as a clear opportunity for improvement. I designed and developed a lightweight, locally running web application to unify all these tasks into a single interface.\n\nThe tool includes multiple timing utilities such as a stopwatch, countdown timer, and airtime calculator. I introduced two operating modes: live mode and recording mode. In live mode, the host can set the program start time and total duration, allowing the system to calculate real-time airtime automatically. In recording mode, the host can structure the entire program in advance while still maintaining accurate timing.\n\nI built a program sheet creator where the host can drag and drop video materials from a media library into the program sequence. Each action automatically updates the total countdown and airtime. A dedicated head option allows the host to measure speaking duration using a stopwatch, with the system recalculating the remaining time instantly. I also added a comments feature so notes or changes related to specific segments can be recorded during the broadcast.\n\nTo complete the workflow, I implemented an export feature that allows the entire program sheet, including timings, video order, and comments, to be downloaded as a PDF. This makes it easy to archive or share the final broadcast plan.\n\nThis project was built using plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The focus was not on frameworks, but on solving a real operational problem through clear logic, usability, and reliability. The tool is actively based on real broadcast workflows and was created by observing actual challenges faced in a control room environment.\n\nWhy this project matters\n\nThis project demonstrates my ability to observe real-time operational challenges, translate them into clear technical requirements, and build a practical solution that improves efficiency. It reflects problem-solving skills, user-focused thinking, and the ability to build tools that support live production environments.",
